From: SM-G955N Ubuntu port contributors
Subject: [PATCH] media: fimc-is2: reject incomplete binary I/O

A filesystem binary read returning EOF (zero) before the advertised size
freed the newly allocated buffer but returned zero to its caller. The caller
could then treat bin->data == NULL and bin->size == 0 as a successfully loaded
image. Normalize zero/positive short reads to EIO and preserve negative errno.
Do not free caller-owned buffers or change their descriptor on failure.

The write helper already retries positive partial writes, but returned success
even after an error or zero-progress write. Return the original negative error
or EIO for zero progress. Keep a zero-length write a successful no-op.

No firmware names, binary contents, calibration selection, loading fallback,
permissions or partition writes are added. This corrects the binary helpers'
I/O result contract; it is not a fix for the observed ISP load EINVAL, which
requires separately validating the stock firmware-selection lifecycle.
Based on pinned Samsung source; no camera streaming or hardware test claimed.

diff --git a/drivers/media/platform/exynos/fimc-is2/fimc-is-binary.c b/drivers/media/platform/exynos/fimc-is2/fimc-is-binary.c
--- a/drivers/media/platform/exynos/fimc-is2/fimc-is-binary.c
+++ b/drivers/media/platform/exynos/fimc-is2/fimc-is-binary.c
@@ -64,7 +64,10 @@
 	if (ret != size) {
 		if (!bin->data)
 			bin->free(buf);
-		return ret;
+		/* EOF and short reads are not a complete binary. In particular,
+		 * returning zero here falsely reported an unset bin as loaded.
+		 */
+		return ret < 0 ? ret : -EIO;
 	}
 
 	bin->data = buf;
@@ -79,6 +82,9 @@
 	ssize_t count;
 	loff_t pos = 0;
 	char *buf = (char *)bin->data;
+
+	if (!bin->size)
+		return 0;
 
 	do {
 		count = min_t(ssize_t, PAGE_SIZE, bin->size - pos);
@@ -87,7 +93,8 @@
 			pos += ret;
 			buf += ret;
 		} else {
-			break;
+			/* Preserve the filesystem error; zero progress cannot finish. */
+			return ret < 0 ? ret : -EIO;
 		}
 	} while (pos < bin->size);
 
